{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## The documented guarantee is false\n\n`docs/user-guide.md` states, as a SECURITY property of the DC backend:\n\n> `JIRA_PAT` is read from the environment and is **not** accepted from a committed config file, so\n> the credential cannot be checked into a repo by accident; the transport never logs it, and **a\n> missing `JIRA_PAT` fails with an error naming the variable rather than falling back to anonymous\n> access.**\n\nThe first two clauses hold. The third does not.\n\n## Mechanism, read from the code\n\n* `adapters/jira_datacenter/settings.py:76` — `pat=os.environ.get(\"JIRA_PAT\", \"\")`. Absent variable\n  yields the EMPTY STRING. There is no validation anywhere that the PAT is non-empty.\n* `adapters/jira_datacenter/transport.py:95` — `jira_cls(server=settings.url,\n  token_auth=settings.pat, options=...)`. The empty string is passed as the bearer token. The client\n  constructs successfully, and requests go out with no usable credential, so Jira treats the caller as\n  ANONYMOUS.\n\n## Observed live — GitHub Actions run 30652534806, job \"jira DC harness smoke\"\n\n`test_the_documented_setup_refuses_to_read_the_pat_from_config` removes `JIRA_PAT` from the\nenvironment (leaving it only in the config file, which must NOT be honoured) and runs the documented\n`rebar reconcile --dry-run`. Expected: a failure naming `JIRA_PAT`. Actual:\n\n    ERROR: reconcile_once raised: HTTP Error 400: JiraError HTTP 400\n      url: .../rest/api/2/search?jql=project+%3D+RBJUXFD+AND+...\n      response text = {\"errorMessages\":[\"The value 'RBJUXFD' does not exist for the field 'project'.\"]}\n\nJira returns exactly \"does not exist for the field 'project'\" when the caller lacks Browse permission\n— it deliberately does not distinguish absent-from-forbidden, to avoid leaking project existence. So\nan anonymous request against a real project produces precisely this message.\n\nCorroborating evidence from the SAME run: `test_the_documented_config_and_dry_run_work_against_a_real_dc_instance`\nPASSED against the same harness, same fixture, same scratch-project machinery, with `JIRA_PAT` SET.\nThe only difference between the passing and failing case is the credential.\n\n## Why this matters beyond tidiness\n\n1. **The failure MISATTRIBUTES, in the most expensive possible direction.** An operator who forgets to\n   export `JIRA_PAT` is told their PROJECT does not exist. They will go and check project keys,\n   permissions and JQL — everything except the credential. This is the same misattribution class that\n   cost this epic a debugging session on the PAT-cap bug, where the error surfaced in an untouched\n   module.\n2. **It is a documented SECURITY guarantee that nothing enforces.** The guide is telling operators\n   that rebar fails closed on a missing credential. It fails OPEN, to anonymous.\n3. **An anonymous pass can look benign.** On an instance where anonymous CAN browse the project, a\n   credential-less run would not error at all — it would read a partial or empty view and report a\n   converged pass. That is the \"green but broken\" shape this epic keeps finding.\n\n## Approach\n\nValidate the PAT at CLIENT-CONSTRUCTION time (`build_client_from_settings`) and fail with an\nactionable error naming the variable — the behaviour the guide already promises.\n\n**REVISED after CI falsified the first placement.** An earlier draft put the check in\n`resolve_jira_datacenter_settings` and rejected client-construction on the reasoning that \"the\ninvariant belongs where the value is resolved\". That reasoning was wrong, and the evidence is\nconcrete: resolution is reached from PROPERTIES (`JiraDataCenterBackend.query_project`), and on\nPython <= 3.11 `isinstance(x, SomeRuntimeCheckableProtocol)` evaluates properties via `hasattr`. A\nraise there breaks every Protocol conformance check. Python 3.12+ switched to\n`inspect.getattr_static`, which does NOT execute properties — so the breakage is INVISIBLE on a\nmodern local interpreter and appears only on the CI matrix's 3.11 leg. Measured: two backend-facade\ntests (`test_isinstance_backend_facade[jira-datacenter]`,\n`test_dc_backend_satisfies_the_backend_facade`) passed on 3.14 locally and failed on 3.11 in CI run\n30653881576, earning a Verified -1.\n\nClient construction is strictly better anyway: it is the LAST point before the network, it is the one\nplace an anonymous client can come into existence, and it cannot be reached by an attribute probe.\nSettings resolution therefore stays TOTAL — it must not raise merely because a credential is absent —\nand a regression test asserts that via `hasattr` on the property, so the guarantee holds regardless of\nwhich interpreter runs the suite.\n\nNote `Backend.assert_env_ready` ALREADY makes this check and names JIRA_PAT, but it is only reached\nfrom the bootstrap-band path (`_attestation.py:68`), which is why dry-run and ordinary reconcile\npasses still went anonymous.\n\nThe error must name `JIRA_PAT` explicitly and say it is environment-only, because the most likely\nmistake is putting it in the config file — which is exactly what the guide forbids and what this\ntest simulates.\n\n**Rejected: validating at settings resolution.** It is the more obvious home and was the first\nattempt; it breaks Protocol conformance checks on Python <= 3.11 for the reason above. Any future\nmove back must first explain how `query_project` stays probe-safe.\n\n**Rejected: allowing an empty PAT for read-only/anonymous instances.** Anonymous access is not a\nsupported deployment for this backend (the guide's least-privilege section mandates a dedicated\nservice account), and the failure mode above shows an anonymous run is indistinguishable from a\nworking one on a permissive instance.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira_datacenter/transport.py` — reject an\n  empty/missing PAT in `build_client_from_settings`.\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira_datacenter/settings.py` — keep resolution TOTAL\n  (a comment records why the guard is not here).\n- `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/` — unit coverage for the new guard.\n\n## Testing\n\n* **unit** — `resolve_jira_datacenter_settings` with `JIRA_PAT` unset raises an error whose message\n  contains `JIRA_PAT`; with it set to whitespace, likewise; with a real value, resolves normally.\n* **unit** — the error is raised at RESOLUTION, before any client is constructed or any network call\n  is attempted (asserted by a client factory that fails the test if invoked).\n* **live** — `test_the_documented_setup_refuses_to_read_the_pat_from_config`, already written and\n  currently FAILING in the harness job, passes. That test is the regression oracle and it was authored\n  before this defect was known, so it is not fitted to the fix.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] with `JIRA_PAT` absent, BUILDING THE DC CLIENT fails with an error whose text contains\n      `JIRA_PAT` and names the anonymous-fallback consequence, asserted by unit test\n- [ ] a whitespace-only `JIRA_PAT` is rejected the same way, since an accidental empty export is the\n      same failure with a different shape\n- [ ] the failure happens BEFORE any network call, asserted by a client factory that fails the test\n      if it is invoked\n- [ ] settings resolution stays TOTAL — it does not raise on a missing credential — asserted the way\n      Python 3.11 exercises it (`hasattr` on `query_project`), so `isinstance(backend, Backend)` keeps\n      working on the 3.11 CI leg. This is the specific regression that earned a Verified -1\n- [ ] a PAT present ONLY in the config file does not satisfy the check — the env-only contract the\n      guide promises is preserved\n- [ ] the live `test_the_documented_setup_refuses_to_read_the_pat_from_config` passes in the\n      `jira DC harness smoke` job, read from the JOB log\n- [ ] the guide's wording and the code now agree; no doc change should be needed, so if one IS made\n      it must be because the code cannot deliver the promise, and that must be stated explicitly"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785522937728152001,"uuid":"088f886d-6828-414d-a0a6-b0c288eee161"}